<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Bolts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a>–<a href="/years/1808">1808</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Bolts was a Dutch-born merchant and former servant of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> after quarrels with its government. He persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> to back a trading venture to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and Asia, and in 1777 founded the short-lived Austrian settlement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bolts was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> on 7 February 1739 and baptised in the English Reformed Church there on 21 February; his parents were William and Sarah Bolts. He later claimed to be a subject of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> through his father, a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, though he said so while trying to escape legal trouble with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at the age of 15 and spent some time in Portugal in the diamond trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a factor of the Company in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> he learned Bengali besides English, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, German, Portuguese and French. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> factory he opened a woollens mart, developed saltpetre manufacture, set up opium plantations, imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and traded in diamonds from the mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>. In 1768 he clashed with the Company, perhaps because diamonds let its servants send home the profits of forbidden private trade; after he announced a newspaper for Calcutta, he was ordered to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and was declared bankrupt.</p><p>His book Considerations on India Affairs of 1772 attacked the Company's arbitrary rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and was translated into French. In 1775 he proposed to the government of Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> a revival of Austrian trade with India from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, and on 24 September 1776 he sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> in the Giuseppe e Teresa with a ten-year imperial charter, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> banker Charles Proli. He set up factories on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a>, by leave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicobar-islands">Nicobar Islands</a>.</p><p>A Portuguese force of 500 men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> removed his men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a> in April 1781, and Danish authorities sent a warship to remove the Austrians from the Nicobars in 1783. The venture made a loss, and after disputes with Proli, Bolts surrendered his charter in August 1781 to the new Imperial Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, which went bankrupt in 1785.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bolts then put a plan for a fur-trading voyage to the North West Coast of America to Joseph II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand IV of Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI of France</a>. The French government adopted the idea, though not its author, in the expedition of Jean-François de Galaup, comte de Lapérouse, and paid him 1,200 Louis. A Swedish plan of 1786 for a colony off Western Australia was shelved. He made his last will in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in August 1805 and died, it is said, in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> poorhouse in 1808.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Bolts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>William Bolts</b>, a former East India Company servant, persuaded the Austrian government of Maria Theresa to back a trading venture to the East under the imperial flag.</blockquote><a href="/state/austrian-delagoa-bay">Austrian Delagoa Bay</a></li></ul></section>
